%I A180207 #15 Nov 15 2026 22:58:27
%S A180207 1,2,4,8,16,34,82,252,890,3644,15390,67646,301210,1397898,6786792,
%T A180207 35154332,193009094,1125920768,6895579278,44276337188,295719612912,
%U A180207 2055385215352
%N A180207 Number of permutations of 0..(n-1) representable as consecutive sums of 4 adjacent elements of a sequence of n+3 nonnegative integers.
%H A180207 Carlo Corti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24194032">Exact Enumeration of Four-Window Permutations: OEIS A180207 through n=22</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A180207 From _Carlo Corti_, Nov 13 2026: (Start)
%F A180207 For a finite set S of positive integers, let k = |S| and let F(S) count the orderings (v(1),...,v(k)) of S for which x(j) >= 0 for 1 <= j <= k, where x(-2) = x(-1) = x(0) = 0 and x(j) = v(j) - x(j-1) - x(j-2) - x(j-3). Set F({}) = 1.
%F A180207 a(n) = Sum_{S subseteq U} F(S)*F(U \ S), where U = {1,...,n-1}, for n >= 1.
%F A180207 a(n+1) >= 2*a(n), and 2^(n-1) <= a(n) <= n!, for n >= 1.
%F A180207 a(n) == 0 (mod 2) for n > 1. (End)
%e A180207 For n = 6, F(S) = 1 for every proper subset S of {1,2,3,4,5}, while F({1,2,3,4,5}) = 2. Thus a(6) = 30 + 2*2 = 34. - _Carlo Corti_, Nov 13 2026
%Y A180207 Cf. A180205, A180206, A180208, A180209, A180210, A180211, A180212.
%K A180207 nonn,more,changed
%O A180207 1,2
